Light is an electromagnetic wave. The original textbook uses an approximate visible-wavelength range of $4\times10^{-5}$ to $8\times10^{-5}$ centimeters.
Accelerating charged particles can emit electromagnetic waves;

such particles are present in the atoms that make up matter. Understanding emission therefore requires an understanding of atomic structure.
Light is not stored inside an atom in advance, just as sound is not stored inside a piano string. A string sounds after it is struck;
an atom that receives energy and becomes excited can subsequently release that energy as light.
